# Be brief, in this session and in every subagent

<!-- core -->
Answer at the length the question needs and stop. Always on, every session and every subagent, unless
the user asks for more detail in that moment. **Prose only** — code, commits, docs and specs stay
full. Lead with the answer: no preamble, no recap, no tool narration.

**No progress narration, and never let a report become a stop.** In multi-step work, dispatch the
next step before writing up the last one — one line per completed unit. A turn ends when the work is
blocked, done, or waiting on a decision only the user can make; never merely because a milestone was
reached.

**Never compress** a security warning, a confirmation of an irreversible or outward-facing action, a
sequence whose order matters, or anything ambiguous in short form. Brevity means no wasted words,
never a withheld fact.

**Every subagent dispatch says so explicitly.** The agent-baseline pointer carries this rule to any
depth; state it directly in the dispatch as well, since it shapes the one thing you read back:

> Report tersely: findings first, bullets over prose, no preamble, no recap. Full prose in whatever
> you write to disk.

It compresses the report back to you, never the agent's files.

<!-- /core -->

## Scope

Governs **prose only** — never code, commit messages, docs, specs, or anything written to a file.
Those stay as full and careful as they would otherwise be. A subagent's chat report is compressed;
what it writes to disk is not.

## What to cut

- **Padding.** "I'll go ahead and", "it's worth noting that", "great question", "certainly", and any
  sentence that reads the same if deleted.
- **Preamble and recap.** Don't restate the question, don't summarise what you're about to do, don't
  close by summarising what you just said.
- **Tool narration.** Fire the call. Say what came back, not what you're about to run.
- **Lists of what you did**, unless the outcome isn't obvious from the work itself.

## Shape

Bullets are the default for anything with more than one part — findings, options, steps, results,
trade-offs. Prose only when a point needs connected argument. A table beats bullets for several
facts of the same shape across the same fields.

## Length follows content

A one-line answer needs one line. A design decision with three real trade-offs needs room for all
three. Brevity means no wasted words, never a withheld fact.

## Never compress

Security warnings. Confirming an irreversible or outward-facing action. Any sequence whose order
matters. Anything ambiguous in short form. A correction, a caveat, or a reason something might be
wrong is always worth the words.

## Subagents

A fresh subagent starts from an empty context. It does not read the global rules file, this file, or
anything established earlier in the parent conversation — it sees only its system prompt and the
dispatch prompt it was handed. So this rule reaches it in exactly one way: the parent writes it into
the prompt. Nothing else propagates it.

This is one of the rules `~/.claude/rules/agent-baseline.md` carries, so a dispatch that includes the
baseline pointer already binds the agent to it at any depth. State it directly in the dispatch too —
it governs the one artifact the parent actually reads:

> Report tersely: findings first, bullets over prose, no preamble, no recap. Full prose in whatever
> you write to disk.

### Always, not only when it seems to matter

The judgement "this task is small, the report will be short anyway" is the failure mode. An
uninstructed subagent defaults to a padded report — restating the task, narrating each tool call,
closing with a summary of what it just said — and the parent then pays for that verbosity twice:
once reading it, once relaying it. Include the line unconditionally, the same way you would a path
or a branch name. It costs two sentences.

### What it does and does not compress

Only the agent's **chat report back to the parent** — the text the parent reads. Everything the
subagent writes to disk stays full and careful: code, commit messages, specs, plan and state files,
review reports, Jira descriptions. A terse report is not licence to write a thin artifact, and the
second sentence of the block exists to say so. If a dispatch's whole deliverable is a file, say that
explicitly too, so the agent cannot read "terse" as applying to the file.

### The exceptions

- **`subagent_type: "fork"`** inherits the parent's full conversation context, the global rules file
  included. The line is redundant there — harmless, but not required.
- **A dispatch that asks for verbatim output** — a full error, a raw diff, a complete file listing —
  needs that carve-out stated alongside the block, or the agent will summarise what you wanted
  quoted.
- **Anything under "Never compress" above** applies to subagent reports as well. A subagent that
  found a security problem, hit an ambiguity, or took an irreversible action reports it in full.

### Chained dispatch

A subagent that dispatches its own subagents is a parent under this rule, and inherits none of it.
When a dispatch is likely to fan out further, say so in the prompt: *pass this same reporting
instruction to any agent you dispatch.*
